1
00:00:18,480 --> 00:00:19,280
Please help me.

2
00:00:19,520 --> 00:00:20,644
Welcome, Jeff Dean.

3
00:00:28,686 --> 00:00:28,996
Thank you.

4
00:00:32,590 --> 00:00:33,120
All right.

5
00:00:33,902 --> 00:00:34,788
Thank you very much.

6
00:00:35,280 --> 00:00:36,420
Thank you for having me.

7
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:44,932
What I'm going to do today is talk to you about what I see as some of the interesting trends and developments in deep learning research.

8
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:55,620
I'm going to be talking about a lot of work done not just by myself, but by many, many people at Google, in particular, lots of work by the Google Brain team and by others at Google.

9
00:00:57,070 --> 00:01:08,080
So deep learning has been pretty successful recently because it is this very universal way of learning to accomplish various tasks.

10
00:01:08,240 --> 00:01:20,560
You can take in very raw forms of data, and these systems can automatically build high-level representations of that data in a way that is driven by the task you're trying to accomplish.

11
00:01:20,942 --> 00:01:23,680
You learn features that are useful for whatever task it is.

12
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:29,188
In this case, we're showing a very simple thing where you take in a photo and try to predict whether it's a cat or a dog.

13
00:01:29,646 --> 00:01:34,960
And low-level features get learned that are sort of, you know, is there an edge at different orientations?

14
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:44,720
And then higher-level features get built up that are combinations of those features until at the very tops of these models you have very detailed, complex high-level features.

15
00:01:44,800 --> 00:01:50,148
Things like is that a, you know, a bunch of face with whiskers or a bunch of ears?

16
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:56,160
And the way these models are trained is by observing examples.

17
00:01:56,302 --> 00:02:02,628
You know, you get an input and you have a label typically for supervised learning that says what the desired output is.

18
00:02:03,374 --> 00:02:16,196
And when your model makes an error, you can do backpropagation, which essentially means making little adjustments to the weights throughout the model so that the next time you see this example, you're a little bit more likely to get that example correct.

19
00:02:16,622 --> 00:02:18,480
And the training process is very iterative.

20
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:27,120
You just make passes over data until you've uh sort of done a good job of fitting the training set that you have.

21
00:02:27,598 --> 00:02:29,508
So nomads are not a new thing.

22
00:02:30,240 --> 00:02:39,172
In, you know, they had a sort of a flash of fashion in the 1980s and 1990s when they showed a lot of promising results on very small problems.

23
00:02:39,726 --> 00:02:43,300
But they really weren't able to scale to large-scale problems.

24
00:02:43,758 --> 00:02:48,356
And so other approaches at that time sort of won out for doing machine learning.

25
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:57,572
but if you fast forward to today, one of the things that's happened is we now have computers that are a thousand times as fast as we had 15, 20 years ago.

26
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:01,700
And that now makes neural networks actually a very attractive approach.

27
00:03:02,000 --> 00:03:20,880
The training process is very computationally intensive, but when you have a very large data set and when you have a large amount of compute, neural nets in many, many domains now are showing that they are the best approach that we know about to achieve success in whatever task that we're trying to accomplish.

28
00:03:21,040 --> 00:03:22,500
And I'll show you some examples of that.

29
00:03:23,470 --> 00:03:34,980
Notice that if you continue going to the right, my belief is that if we're able to train larger and larger models on larger data sets, we'll actually widen the gap between neural nets and other approaches.

30
00:03:36,174 --> 00:03:44,388
So the field of computer vision is one that's been really revolutionized in the last just four or five years by neural nets.

31
00:03:45,358 --> 00:03:51,424
So in 2011, the ImageNet Challenge winner achieved an error rate of 26%.

32
00:03:52,718 --> 00:03:59,984
Um and if you compare that with human accuracy on this task, which is to assign one of a thousand labels to a photograph, like what is that?

33
00:04:00,064 --> 00:04:05,220
Is that a truck or a garbage truck or a fireboat or a cheetah?

34
00:04:06,766 --> 00:04:09,252
computers in 2011 were pretty terrible at this.

35
00:04:09,838 --> 00:04:11,428
Humans were much, much better.

36
00:04:11,822 --> 00:04:17,664
But if you look today, just five years later, we're actually better than humans at this particular task.

37
00:04:17,966 --> 00:04:23,972
Now, this is not to say we're better than humans at all computer vision tasks, but we're actually better than humans at the ImageNet Challenge.

38
00:04:24,622 --> 00:04:25,904
And that's pretty amazing, right?

39
00:04:26,064 --> 00:04:31,076
We've gone from a very fuzzy, blurry view of the world to a pretty crisp view.

40
00:04:31,758 --> 00:04:38,660
And if you think about the time in evolution when animals evolve dies, that's sort of where we are now in computer vision.

41
00:04:38,990 --> 00:04:47,984
Now that we can see, that's really going to enable a tremendous amount of other kinds of things that are downstream of just being able to observe and understand the world.

42
00:04:49,422 --> 00:04:53,956
So one of the really great things about deep learning is that it's not just for vision.

43
00:04:54,478 --> 00:04:56,944
It's useful for many, many kinds of tasks.

44
00:04:57,104 --> 00:05:03,780
Language understanding, speech recognition, you know, many tasks all rely on the same fundamental algorithms.

45
00:05:04,464 --> 00:05:14,224
And so when our team started, we started playing with a computer vision application and a speech application, and that's sort of what you see in the 2012 time frame.

46
00:05:14,464 --> 00:05:16,676
We started collaborating with a few groups at Google.

47
00:05:17,038 --> 00:05:24,704
And then as we showed that this approach would work for different kinds of problems, other teams would say, hey, I want to try this on my problem.

48
00:05:25,104 --> 00:05:31,268
And we had this really nice exponential growth of teams using deep learning for lots of different tasks.

49
00:05:31,984 --> 00:05:37,220
And building the right tools causes that to spread really rapidly within Google.

50
00:05:37,518 --> 00:05:41,984
You know, now many, many teams are you know fluent in using these approaches.

51
00:05:42,094 --> 00:05:47,024
They're using it for not just one problem in their domain, but many problems, and so on.

52
00:05:47,184 --> 00:05:57,796
So I think this is really a big transformation in how people approach solving kind of difficult problems that traditionally we didn't really know how to solve in other ways.

53
00:05:59,534 --> 00:06:00,704
So a little bit about our team.

54
00:06:00,878 --> 00:06:14,148
So our team is a research team, and we publish papers in machine learning conferences, things like ICML and NIPS and ICLR and other venues related to natural language processing and computer systems.

55
00:06:14,704 --> 00:06:18,224
We run a lot of sort of research training programs.

56
00:06:18,464 --> 00:06:24,224
we run a program called the Brain Residency Program, which allows people to come spend a year in our group to learn how to do deep learning research.

57
00:06:24,304 --> 00:06:32,676
We hosted 50 interns in 2016, but we also collaborate with product teams to get the results of our research into products.

58
00:06:33,294 --> 00:06:49,732
So we've had a lot of collaborations and we're now helping push deep learning into, you know, it's run on every query at Google, it's run to select ads, it's used in the Google Photos product, as I'll show you, and the translate product as I'll discuss in a minute.

59
00:06:50,224 --> 00:06:51,972
Many, many places are now using this.

60
00:06:52,686 --> 00:06:54,980
And we also build open source tools.

61
00:06:55,824 --> 00:07:00,064
the best known is TensorFlow, but we also open source a lot of the results of our research.

62
00:07:00,144 --> 00:07:07,104
So we have companion code published now with a lot of our research papers so people can reproduce our results, can build on them, and can extend them.

63
00:07:07,406 --> 00:07:14,340
And that really just helps spread research ideas throughout the ML community, both within Google and outside.

64
00:07:16,462 --> 00:07:21,732
So one of the things we realized when we first started the project was we needed to build the right tools.

65
00:07:22,384 --> 00:07:27,876
And really that enables you to do research more rapidly and more quickly.

66
00:07:28,384 --> 00:07:37,284
And so we actually built an earlier system where we didn't know the answers to these questions, and we solved some of these issues.

67
00:07:37,664 --> 00:07:41,572
our earlier system was called disbelief, and it was not open source, but we wrote a paper about it.

68
00:07:42,224 --> 00:07:46,864
but if you step back and think about what you want in a machine learning system, you really want all of these attributes.

69
00:07:46,958 --> 00:07:50,624
You want to be able to easily express new machine learning ideas.

70
00:07:51,406 --> 00:07:58,980
You'd like things to scale because a lot of the problems we want to deal with have large underlying data sets, and you need to train very large models on these things.

71
00:08:00,334 --> 00:08:15,140
it's nice if it's portable so that you can take a system, train it on you know a desktop machine, maybe with some GPU cards or a data center, but then deploy it in a s in a smartphone so that you can do speech recognition on device without needing a network connection, for example.

72
00:08:16,238 --> 00:08:25,092
reproducibility is important, so being able to share the underlying representation of your code and algorithms so that other people can build on it is important.

73
00:08:25,390 --> 00:08:26,624
And then production readiness.

74
00:08:26,704 --> 00:08:34,180
You want to go from a research idea to a production system without having to sort of re implement things in a fundamental way.

75
00:08:36,576 --> 00:08:40,356
and so the second system that we've built is a system called TensorFlow.

76
00:08:40,976 --> 00:09:00,580
And we actually open sourced this a little more than a year ago under a very flexible Apache 2.0 license, because we wanted to be able to share our TensorFlow uh programs and research with the outside community, and also have this platform be something that many, many people around the world can use.

77
00:09:01,998 --> 00:09:07,856
and so it's available on GitHub, there's a website that has documentation and so on.

78
00:09:08,016 --> 00:09:17,988
For example, one of the things that I think has made TensorFlow reasonably successful is we've put together a bunch of tutorials that show how to use TensorFlow to accomplish various machine learning tasks.

79
00:09:18,176 --> 00:09:26,532
Things like computer vision, there's an example, machine translation system as a tutorial there, very simple things like doing language modeling.

80
00:09:27,502 --> 00:09:34,660
and these tutorials walk you through both the machine learning ideas and also how to express them in TensorFlow.

81
00:09:35,182 --> 00:09:51,940
And I think that's been a reasonably successful and accessible way for people who maybe aren't as familiar with the machine learning research literature to dive in and start understanding what these techniques can accomplish, to look at problems they might have and see how they might map to some of these tutorials and ideas.

82
00:09:52,590 --> 00:09:55,012
So that's been reasonably successful, I think.

83
00:09:56,558 --> 00:10:03,524
And as I said, the reason we built TensorFlow was we wanted a system that was flexible, scalable, and production ready.

84
00:10:04,110 --> 00:10:11,268
Our first system was essentially scalable and production ready and good for certain kinds of models, but not as flexible as we wanted.

85
00:10:11,662 --> 00:10:20,164
As we expanded the repertoire of kinds of models we were exploring, it turned out we weren't easily able to express some kinds of models in there.

86
00:10:20,462 --> 00:10:32,964
And so that's why we sort of took what we learned from our first system and built TensorFlow to be scalable and production ready, retain those attributes, but also be flexible and a little bit more general purpose.

87
00:10:33,936 --> 00:10:46,256
other existing open source packages that we looked at around the time we were developing TensorFlow were also good on two of the three, but not all three, and they differed in which kinds of two of the attributes they were sort of really strong in.

88
00:10:47,758 --> 00:10:58,496
And so our goals in TensorFlow were to establish this common platform for expressing machine learning ideas and hopefully make it the best in the world for both research and production use.

89
00:10:58,656 --> 00:11:05,284
We think both of those are important to our group, and we think they're they're of growing importance to members of the community.

90
00:11:05,998 --> 00:11:08,932
And open source it, of course, so that it becomes a platform for everyone.

91
00:11:10,062 --> 00:11:12,016
So we launched it a little more than a year ago.

92
00:11:12,270 --> 00:11:19,696
The initial launch was reasonably full featured, so it has features like auto differentiation, the inspiration for which we took from Theano.

93
00:11:20,336 --> 00:11:29,936
we had a system for cues so that you can have multiple asynchronous pieces of computation that are communicating through these asynchronous cues.

94
00:11:30,096 --> 00:11:37,476
We have support for control flow operations in the TensorFlow model descriptions, and a pretty comprehensive set of operations.

95
00:11:38,126 --> 00:11:40,816
And the tutorials I think made the system pretty accessible.

96
00:11:41,198 --> 00:11:45,956
You know, we out of the box we supported CPUs, GPUs, many different kinds of devices, many platforms.

97
00:11:48,110 --> 00:11:50,468
and some stats as of a few days ago.

98
00:11:50,816 --> 00:11:56,004
We have more than 500 contributors to the TensorFlow code base, most of them outside Google.

99
00:11:56,558 --> 00:12:00,016
We've had 12,000 commits since November 2015.

100
00:12:00,416 --> 00:12:01,456
that's quite a large number.

101
00:12:01,536 --> 00:12:05,412
That's about 30 per day going into our shared platform.

102
00:12:06,096 --> 00:12:09,604
about a million people have downloaded more than a million people have downloaded the binary platform.

103
00:12:09,902 --> 00:12:13,136
It's now the 15th most popular repository on GitHub by stars.

104
00:12:13,296 --> 00:12:18,244
We we just passed Linux, which we're you know pretty, pretty proud of, and not bad for a year.

105
00:12:19,310 --> 00:12:29,220
and it's now being used as kind of the underlying mechanism for teaching machine learning in several different university classes at places like Toronto and Berkeley and Stanford.

106
00:12:29,870 --> 00:12:37,412
And lots of different organizations have now adopted TensorFlow as the way they do machine learning research and production.

107
00:12:38,944 --> 00:12:43,504
The other nice thing is this community really has grown around TensorFlow.

108
00:12:43,598 --> 00:12:54,244
So the TensorFlow repository you see there at the top, this is a GitHub search, but there's 5,000 repositories, more than 5,000 repositories about TensorFlow, most of which are totally unaffiliated with us.

109
00:12:54,384 --> 00:12:57,828
They're all people expressing different kinds of models, trying out things.

110
00:12:58,464 --> 00:13:02,884
There's various tutorials, people have been translating a lot of the documentation into other languages.

111
00:13:03,854 --> 00:13:10,660
And this is the community that we have really enjoyed helping to foster and we're really seeing it thrive.

112
00:13:14,638 --> 00:13:20,996
Since our initial open sourcing, we've added a repository for reference implementations of common models.

113
00:13:21,390 --> 00:13:23,184
So there's a whole bunch of them in there now.

114
00:13:23,504 --> 00:13:32,260
this is reasonably popular, and you can do things like you know, autoencoders and image to text, so you can caption images, you can do language modeling, neural GPUs.

115
00:13:32,686 --> 00:13:35,984
A lot of the research work from our group is there.

116
00:13:36,224 --> 00:13:47,428
A lot of reference implementations of other models are in there, and we definitely encourage contributions there, and you should look there if you want to build on one of these things in your own work.

117
00:13:48,784 --> 00:13:53,636
we've also kept up a pretty good cadence of releases of the TensorFlow system.

118
00:13:54,624 --> 00:14:03,172
So a month later we added support for various versions of Python, we made it faster in GPUs, we added QDNN support and dynamic loading of kernels.

119
00:14:03,744 --> 00:14:22,020
a few months later, we added a distributed implementation in the open source release, which we weren't able to get in the initial release because we just hadn't been able to get the open source code free of dependencies at the time of the initial release, but that was a very requested feature and has been in there since April.

120
00:14:22,784 --> 00:14:27,684
we've been adding support for other platforms, so we now have iOS support and GPUs on Macs.

121
00:14:28,544 --> 00:14:35,904
we've added some higher level APIs for allowing people to more concisely specify machine learning models.

122
00:14:36,142 --> 00:14:51,076
So one of the things about the flexibility is you can express things at a very low level, which is great for research, but often for more straightforward uses, you just want concise, high-level ways of saying, I just like a five-layer neural net with these sort of properties.

123
00:14:51,374 --> 00:14:54,340
And we've added support for that in various ways.

124
00:14:55,104 --> 00:15:00,228
we've added support for HDFS, including NV5 as these things come out.

125
00:15:00,704 --> 00:15:10,944
we've added some nice visualization support, we've added Windows support, which was pretty heavily requested, added support for other language bindings, including Go.

126
00:15:12,590 --> 00:15:15,344
and I'm just showing you the highlights for each of those releases.

127
00:15:15,424 --> 00:15:28,292
Each release has actually a whole bunch of sort of more modest features and improvements, and this is an example of the release notes for version 0.11, which you can find in the GitHub repository.

128
00:15:29,646 --> 00:15:34,064
over time, we and other contributors have added support for a lot of different front ends.

129
00:15:34,224 --> 00:15:41,604
So the Python one is the most fully developed, most people use that, but we use C for a lot of our production uses.

130
00:15:42,144 --> 00:15:45,220
lots of people are starting to use Go and Java.

131
00:15:45,664 --> 00:15:47,504
there's Haskell and R support now.

132
00:15:47,694 --> 00:15:52,068
So I expect this suite to continue to expand in terms of supported languages.

133
00:15:52,944 --> 00:15:54,304
version one is upcoming.

134
00:15:54,384 --> 00:15:57,988
We actually just released an Alpha Candidate 1.0 release yesterday.

135
00:15:58,464 --> 00:16:12,196
that's going to have backwards compatibility guarantees, whereas when we were in the zero point version numbering system, we didn't want to provide those guarantees, but now that we're a 1.0 release, we're going to do that.

136
00:16:12,544 --> 00:16:16,384
we'll focus a lot on usability in improving the documentation.

137
00:16:16,464 --> 00:16:21,584
I think it's already pretty good and it's been improved quite a bit for the 1.0 release.

138
00:16:22,574 --> 00:16:25,264
And we've published a couple of white papers.

139
00:16:25,824 --> 00:16:39,396
well, the top one is a white paper describing the general overall system design, and the bottom one is a scientific systems research paper that appeared in OSDI about kind of some of the underlying systems decisions and measurements of TensorFlow.

140
00:16:41,806 --> 00:16:48,064
And as I said, we've had pretty good response for external adoption of TensorFlow.

141
00:16:48,224 --> 00:16:55,844
And that's it compared to a bunch of the other sort of similar frameworks for implementing deep learning research.

142
00:16:56,384 --> 00:16:59,584
and you notice the launch dates of various packages there.

143
00:16:59,904 --> 00:17:01,764
TensorFlow is the youngest of all these.

144
00:17:05,024 --> 00:17:15,172
so one of the things we focus a lot on in our group is being able to take an idea and a data set and train a model as fast as we possibly can.

145
00:17:15,470 --> 00:17:17,956
There's a very different feel when you're doing research.

146
00:17:18,158 --> 00:17:25,104
If you can train a model in, you know, ideally minutes, but more realistically for large deep learning problems, hours.

147
00:17:25,264 --> 00:17:28,900
That's a very different feeling than something that takes you a week to train.

148
00:17:29,358 --> 00:17:48,740
And so by speeding up the iteration time, we're actually able to improve our quality of research because we're able to do more experiments, learn from the set of experiments we initially do, and then you know diverge in whatever appropriate direction based on those experimental results to go into the next set of experiments.

149
00:17:49,880 --> 00:17:56,868
And so, you know, essentially we have a patience threshold, and we're going to train the largest model we possibly can in that patience threshold.

150
00:17:57,230 --> 00:17:59,684
Everyone's patience threshold is slightly different.

151
00:18:00,014 --> 00:18:03,812
Most people's longest one is about a few days and not much longer than that.

152
00:18:06,574 --> 00:18:14,596
so one of the things we're excited about in the upcoming 1.0 release is going to be an alpha version of a compilation system for TensorFlow.

153
00:18:14,990 --> 00:18:21,156
So the way a TensorFlow model works is it has this graph, which you see in very small form there on the bottom of the slide.

154
00:18:21,550 --> 00:18:25,316
And the current implementation essentially interprets that graph a node at a time.

155
00:18:26,702 --> 00:18:30,624
It's okay to interpret it because a lot of those nodes are pretty heavyweight things.

156
00:18:30,704 --> 00:18:35,584
They're like do a giant matrix multiply and then feed the results to another giant matrix multiply.

157
00:18:35,758 --> 00:18:51,268
And so the interpretation overhead is not necessarily that large, but for some kinds of models, it's actually really important that we be able to avoid that interpretation overhead and compile a small subgraph of this thing into an optimized kernel for these 20 nodes.

158
00:18:51,854 --> 00:18:55,332
And we can really get much, much better performance by doing this.

159
00:18:56,334 --> 00:19:06,532
so we were adding a system called XLA, a compilation system that's been developed in conjunction with the compiler team at Google to essentially accelerate TensorFlow computations.

160
00:19:07,264 --> 00:19:24,356
And I will give you TensorFlow graphs go in and we identify small subgraphs of those that can be compiled, and then we generate optimized and specialized assembly for exactly the shapes of tensors that are being manipulated, and I have a small video demo.

161
00:19:25,166 --> 00:19:27,044
Compiler video demos are always exciting.

162
00:19:30,110 --> 00:19:30,980
Oops, let's see here,

163
00:19:35,694 --> 00:19:36,516
I'm not sure what's going on.

164
00:19:37,582 --> 00:19:38,404
that was working.

165
00:19:39,662 --> 00:19:41,220
Let me connect to the wireless.

166
00:20:15,406 --> 00:20:16,100
My apologies.

167
00:20:17,454 --> 00:20:32,868
But essentially it's showing you that for a very simple bit of code here that's trying to multiply two vectors together, two four element vectors, and then produce the result.

168
00:20:34,062 --> 00:20:42,692
it can actually get that down to essentially four assembly instructions, as you would, you know, if you were really writing really tight C code to do this.

169
00:20:43,040 --> 00:20:53,476
it actually can take advantage because it knows that it's on a Intel machine that supports AVX instructions, It can actually do that operation in one, you know, four-element vector operation.

170
00:20:54,080 --> 00:20:56,160
it also can generate code for GPUs.

171
00:20:56,240 --> 00:21:04,580
So if you change that XLA CPU into an XLA GPU, then it would generate optimized assembly code for an NVIDIA GPU card.

172
00:21:05,040 --> 00:21:09,040
And we're seeing reasonable performance gains for real models through the system.

173
00:21:09,262 --> 00:21:10,560
So we're pretty excited about this.

174
00:21:10,640 --> 00:21:25,380
We think it's going to be really nice because one of the things that happens when you're doing sort of research is you compose together a lot of little small operations, and you want those to perform as well as if you'd written sort of a optimized version of that code.

175
00:21:25,710 --> 00:21:33,316
And this will allow you to write the code in the flexible, expressive way that you'd like, but then the compilation system will take care of generating really high quality code.

176
00:21:33,870 --> 00:21:34,756
Okay, hopefully.

177
00:21:35,246 --> 00:21:38,116
Okay, so sorry the video didn't work.

