00:21:38,958 --> 00:21:48,452
I'm going to skip, I'm going to basically talk about some of the ways that deep learning is having a significant impact on Google, Google research, and Google products.

179
00:21:48,782 --> 00:21:56,900
And all of these examples are things that we've implemented in TensorFlow or in our predecessor system in collaboration with product teams at Google in many cases.

180
00:21:58,158 --> 00:22:01,764
So one of the areas that we worked in first was speech recognition.

181
00:22:02,320 --> 00:22:09,476
And I think you'll hear more in the speech session later today about how different companies are using deep neural nets for speech recognition.

182
00:22:09,870 --> 00:22:17,520
The first thing that happened was people replaced the acoustic part of a speech recognition system with a deep neural net.

183
00:22:17,760 --> 00:22:37,280
And that gave very significant improvements in accuracy because those neural nets are actually better able to work in noisy conditions and to generalize better to sort of all kinds of different voice pitches and people speaking and accents, and that reduced the word error rate by a significant amount, 20% or something.

184
00:22:37,360 --> 00:22:50,640
And then over time we've rolled out, and the speech team has rolled out a number of other improvements using neural nets in more fundamental ways across the whole speech recognition pipeline to fundamentally reduce the word error rate by 30%.

185
00:22:50,990 --> 00:22:52,388
And this is really, really important.

186
00:22:54,126 --> 00:23:02,884
this word error rate reduction is equivalent to sort of the previous couple of decades of speech improvement in a lot of production systems.

187
00:23:03,520 --> 00:23:10,180
And that's really made it so that when you talk to your phone, it generally now understands rather than not understands.

188
00:23:10,606 --> 00:23:15,120
And that's fundamental for working on mobile user interfaces.

189
00:23:15,280 --> 00:23:22,640
Having good speech recognition actually really enables a very different kind of experience for how you interact with your mobile device.

190
00:23:24,302 --> 00:23:35,280
Another area now that we can actually do computer vision and can classify photos and understand what's in them, we can actually localize things and understand where different kinds of objects are, is in Google Photos.

191
00:23:35,374 --> 00:23:45,520
One of the fundamental building blocks of the new Google Photos product when it came out, was this ability to understand your pictures at a fairly deep level and categorize them.

192
00:23:45,680 --> 00:23:46,720
cluster them by.

193
00:23:46,800 --> 00:23:53,732
you know, "this is your pictures of all the birthday parties you've ever been to ," and "these are pictures of mountains ,", "these, are pictures of the ocean.

194
00:23:54,126 --> 00:24:03,684
And understanding what's in those photos allows you to easily search them, allows you to easily sort of browse them in flexible and creative ways, rather than just by the date they were taken.

195
00:24:04,174 --> 00:24:15,204
And that's really important to build a product that people really love around a really important uh you know thing in all of our lives with uh photos with us, all cameras with us all the time.

196
00:24:16,782 --> 00:24:20,208
And so here you see, you know, we can cluster by different categories.

197
00:24:20,288 --> 00:24:22,436
We can find your pictures of Siamese cats.

198
00:24:24,110 --> 00:24:26,468
Some of these are maybe only half Siamese, but that's okay.

199
00:24:29,038 --> 00:24:43,332
And one of the things we've found is that when we develop a model to accomplish a certain task, there's often a lot of other very unrelated conceptually unrelated tasks that can actually benefit from that same research.

200
00:24:44,048 --> 00:24:52,484
So as an example, given the same basic model structure, trained on different data, we can then reuse the research we've done in different contexts.

201
00:24:53,550 --> 00:24:59,396
So given an image, for example, there's a lot of problems where you want to predict interesting pixels of that image.

202
00:25:00,928 --> 00:25:07,972
And my summer intern, Matt Zealer, a few years ago and I collaborated with our Street View team to build a model.

203
00:25:08,686 --> 00:25:13,088
In this case, we wanted to be able to take Street View images and find text in them.

204
00:25:13,230 --> 00:25:15,300
This is a precursor to actually reading the text.

205
00:25:16,014 --> 00:25:18,724
You first need to be able to find which pieces of the image are text.

206
00:25:19,374 --> 00:25:24,356
And so you want to predict a heat map of pixels that contain text given an image.

207
00:25:24,846 --> 00:25:37,124
And so with some training data where human operators had drawn, you know, uh polygons around pieces of the image that contained text, we actually could produce a model that did a pretty good job of finding text.

208
00:25:37,614 --> 00:25:47,716
And you know, it worked in different character sets for very big pieces of text, small ones, different colors, farther away from the street, close to the street.

209
00:25:48,942 --> 00:25:56,288
and so that was pretty useful, and then could then feed into the rest of the street view pipeline that's actually trying to do OCR on the regions that do contain text.

210
00:25:57,838 --> 00:26:10,244
So then someone in the maps product area decided to build this project called Sunroof, which is designed to find rooftops that would be amenable to solar energy installations.

211
00:26:10,862 --> 00:26:20,324
And the normal way you get this done is you say, could a solar installer please come out to my house and like stare at my roof and estimate how much solar energy I could generate?

212
00:26:20,750 --> 00:26:34,208
And it turns out you can actually do this with computer vision from satellite imagery, because you can estimate the angle of the roof relative to the sun, and you know where the roof is, you know how much rooftop there is, and you can do this calculation just online.

213
00:26:34,368 --> 00:26:38,148
So you can enter your home address and see if solar panels would make sense for your house.

214
00:26:39,328 --> 00:26:41,408
and this is essentially the same model.

215
00:26:41,488 --> 00:26:46,980
You're trying to predict interesting pixels, which ones contain rooftops, from satellite imagery.

216
00:26:48,558 --> 00:26:53,380
Another area where we've been able to reuse the same general model is in medical imaging.

217
00:26:53,728 --> 00:26:59,588
So here we've been doing work on detecting diabetic retinopathy in retinal images.

218
00:27:00,942 --> 00:27:09,088
and this is really important because there's 400 million people at risk for developing diabetic retinopathy, which can actually cause blindness if it's not treated.

219
00:27:09,168 --> 00:27:11,396
But if it is caught in time, it's actually very treatable.

220
00:27:13,390 --> 00:27:21,648
and the real problem is that in many parts of the world, the actual cameras to take the retinal images are not that in short supply.

221
00:27:21,728 --> 00:27:24,356
It's actually not that expensive a camera.

222
00:27:24,590 --> 00:27:34,756
But the thing that's in short supply is an ophthalmologist to interpret that image and understand is this someone who should go get further treatment or evaluation, or do you not show any signs of this?

223
00:27:35,918 --> 00:27:43,268
And so in a paper published in December of last year in the journal of the American Medical Association, people in our group

224
00:27:47,534 --> 00:27:49,248
my monitor just went off in the front here.

225
00:27:49,408 --> 00:27:49,924
Oh, thank you.

226
00:27:50,128 --> 00:27:50,820
Okay, there it is.

227
00:27:52,448 --> 00:28:10,848
showed that essentially you can train a computer vision model on carefully labeled data sets that have been labeled by board-certified ophthalmologists, and you can actually then have a model that is on par, perhaps slightly better, than the median ophthalmologist at doing this diagnosis task, this detection task.

228
00:28:10,928 --> 00:28:19,248
And that's really important because we think this will enable greater access to the right kind of screening for everyone doing diabetic retinopathy.

229
00:28:19,408 --> 00:28:22,340
And we see similar kinds of problems in many other medical imaging domains.

230
00:28:22,702 --> 00:28:33,252
Now that computer vision works, you know, it's this tool that you can now rethink what is possible using computers or assisted with computers, and we're pretty excited about that.

231
00:28:37,006 --> 00:28:41,156
So another domain that's going to be affected by vision is robotics.

232
00:28:41,678 --> 00:28:48,612
You essentially robots haven't been able to see all that well, and it's really hard to build a robot if you can't see the world in front of you.

233
00:28:49,104 --> 00:29:06,784
But now we can actually see pretty well, and so we can actually build robots that are fundamentally using a computer vision system and then use the output of the computer vision system to directly control the robots.

234
00:29:07,086 --> 00:29:15,972
Essentially, so we in this case we have a robotic lab that we've set up where we can essentially have robots try to learn hand-eye coordination.

235
00:29:16,398 --> 00:29:26,276
So we have a neural net that goes from pixel inputs from those cameras that you see above the robots to directly to six torque motor controls for the different motors on the arm.

236
00:29:26,894 --> 00:29:33,476
And essentially, these arms are learning to pick up objects based on the perceptual inputs they're seeing.

237
00:29:33,744 --> 00:29:41,028
Oh, it's like a long tubular object, so I should use this kind of grip, or it's a short fat object, so I should reach under it and try to grab it that way.

238
00:29:41,664 --> 00:29:52,068
And by trial and error, essentially, it's a supervised task because if they pinch their gripper and they fail to pick something up, their gripper closes all the way, so they can tell if they've succeeded or not.

239
00:29:53,024 --> 00:29:57,540
I'm not going to try to play the video because I think it won't work, but there is a video that you can find online.

240
00:29:58,958 --> 00:30:00,196
just search for that blog post.

241
00:30:00,944 --> 00:30:12,132
and essentially they're able to learn to grasp objects and learn from their successes and failures, and over time they improve tremendously in their ability to successfully grasp objects.

242
00:30:13,006 --> 00:30:21,348
And the other interesting thing is because there are many arms, unlike humans that are trying to learn to do this task, you can actually pool your experience by collecting log files.

243
00:30:21,870 --> 00:30:29,636
And those log files can be aggregated and then train a new model every night on the aggregated experience of these many robotic arms.

244
00:30:30,704 --> 00:30:37,316
and you know, you're learning from the experience of your neighboring robot, which may not be exactly calibrated the same way.

245
00:30:37,550 --> 00:30:43,556
You know, their camera might be in a slightly different position, their motor might be a little stickier in their elbow, just like humans.

246
00:30:44,864 --> 00:30:50,436
and you still get a lot of benefit from observing that experience and learning from it.

247
00:30:53,742 --> 00:30:57,220
So the other area that's pretty exciting is in better language understanding.

248
00:30:58,144 --> 00:31:10,784
And one of the ways that we've been able to do this is to really understand at a deeper level than just the raw, ASCII representations of words what those words mean.

249
00:31:11,054 --> 00:31:16,452
So, for example, here we have a query, car parts for sale, and we have a document that says rebuilt transmissions.

250
00:31:17,134 --> 00:31:21,956
We'd like to actually understand that that's a pretty good match for that query, even though it has no words in common.

251
00:31:22,510 --> 00:31:37,344
And so with a deep neural net, we can actually have these nice kind of smooth representations of words where we know that car parts is actually fairly closely related to things like transmissions and gear shifts and brake pads and things like that.

252
00:31:37,646 --> 00:31:48,324
And we can actually use a deep neural net to give us a score for how likely it is that this document, given the words in the document, is related to this query, the words in that query.

253
00:31:49,198 --> 00:31:51,264
And this was launched in 2015.

254
00:31:51,630 --> 00:31:57,188
It's now the third most important search ranking signal in Google search ranking out of hundreds of signals.

255
00:31:58,144 --> 00:32:02,144
it's a pretty important aspect of our ranking system now.

256
00:32:05,038 --> 00:32:22,724
The other kind of work, so some work that was done in our group originally in the context of an abstract machine learning model for transforming one input sequence to predicting an output sequence given the input sequence.

257
00:32:23,406 --> 00:32:30,468
So essentially you want to condition the generation of an output sequence on the input sequence you've observed.

258
00:32:31,024 --> 00:32:46,660
Now this sounds kind of abstract, but one area this is useful is you'd like to condition the output sequence, in this case, an English sentence, on the observation of this input sequence, in this case, a French sentence.

259
00:32:47,024 --> 00:33:04,740
And so you can essentially produce a machine translation system by observing pairs of French English sentences that mean the same thing, and then learning that when I see these kinds of words in this French sentence, I should use these kinds of words in this position in the English sentence.

260
00:33:05,870 --> 00:33:09,680
And this neural net has no idea that it's actually translating.

261
00:33:09,774 --> 00:33:14,724
It just learns to transform to produce an output sequence given an input sequence.

262
00:33:15,726 --> 00:33:21,348
And so conditioned on the first word that it's generated, it can generate the second word in the output sequence.

263
00:33:22,286 --> 00:33:25,988
Conditioned on the second word, and all the previous words, it can generate the third one, and so on.

264
00:33:26,720 --> 00:33:34,692
And then at inference time, you actually have a probability distribution over likely output sequences given the input sequence.

265
00:33:35,054 --> 00:33:44,100
And so you can do a beam search where you explore the possibilities because the most likely target sentence is not necessarily the one that starts for the most likely word.

266
00:33:47,760 --> 00:33:47,920
no?

267
00:33:48,000 --> 00:33:58,340
This is using an LSTM representation, so a long short-term memory cell, which is originally developed by Sepp Hochreiter and Jürgen Schmidthuber.

268
00:33:59,440 --> 00:34:05,892
And essentially these models are very good at remembering long distance dependencies.

269
00:34:06,446 --> 00:34:12,720
So if I saw an input word 20 words ago, they're good at retaining the knowledge that I saw that word.

270
00:34:14,446 --> 00:34:18,948
So another area we've been able to use this research is in collaboration with the Gmail team.

271
00:34:20,462 --> 00:34:24,676
We all know that typing on mobile devices is a bit of a pain, that's why speech recognition is important.

272
00:34:25,198 --> 00:34:30,080
But it's also good to consider other modalities for making it easier to interact with your device.

273
00:34:30,160 --> 00:34:36,900
And so Smart Reply is, well, originally it was an April Fool's joke that we put out in April 2009.

274
00:34:37,902 --> 00:34:41,252
But then in November 2015, we launched a real product around this.

275
00:34:41,774 --> 00:34:42,692
So I guess that's good.

276
00:34:44,720 --> 00:34:54,640
And what this does is it takes an incoming email message, and then given the text of the message, it tries to predict what are likely responses you might want to give to this message.

277
00:34:54,800 --> 00:35:10,980
And so here you see, this person got this fairly long email, not super long, but long if you have to reply to it on an email, and it predicts a few likely responses, and you can just click on one, you can send it as it is, you can edit it as you see fit.

278
00:35:11,600 --> 00:35:14,160
And this has been really amazing for Gmail.

279
00:35:14,240 --> 00:35:21,508
Now 10% of mobile responses on email, on the inbox product, are generated with this smart reply feature.

280
00:35:21,998 --> 00:35:23,428
So that's saving people a lot of time.

281
00:35:23,982 --> 00:35:26,320
And the way it works is we have an incoming email.

282
00:35:26,478 --> 00:35:28,932
You know, this is one that my teammate received from his brother.

283
00:35:29,326 --> 00:35:34,212
Hey, we wanted to invite you to join us for an early Thanksgiving on November 22nd, blah, blah, blah, blah, blah.

284
00:35:34,542 --> 00:35:41,540
And we have a small neural net that says, is this the kind of thing where we can likely generate a short response that would make sense for this message?

285
00:35:42,062 --> 00:35:51,140
If yes, then we activate a much bigger recurrent model, one of these sequence-to-sequence models, that tries to generate likely replies.

286
00:35:51,374 --> 00:35:54,852
And here it says, count us in, we'll be there, or sorry, we won't be able to make it.

287
00:35:57,294 --> 00:35:59,280
And then you can click on one of those.

288
00:36:02,030 --> 00:36:06,532
We've also been seen cases where we can combine computer vision with language.

289
00:36:07,374 --> 00:36:17,880
And essentially you can take the sequence-to-sequence model, and instead of initializing the input sequence with a sentence, you can initialize it with an image using a deep neural net.

290
00:36:27,118 --> 00:36:29,348
I thought it went till uh 9.50.

291
00:36:31,502 --> 00:36:32,324
Oh, that says three.

292
00:36:34,130 --> 00:36:34,310
OK.

293
00:36:35,406 --> 00:36:37,540
so you can caption images, which is exciting.

294
00:36:38,240 --> 00:36:41,828
And I'm going to skip that, but essentially you can generate nice model captions.

295
00:36:43,040 --> 00:36:45,764
and translation is another area that I think is pretty exciting.

296
00:36:46,560 --> 00:36:55,120
So the sequence-to-sequence work showed initial signs that on a public data set, you could generate good translations.

297
00:36:55,200 --> 00:37:06,180
but our production data sets are much, much bigger, and it was not entirely clear to some of us that it could actually be successfully deployed in a translation product.

298
00:37:07,150 --> 00:37:20,640
and actually, in a collaboration with the translate team, we rolled out a translation system based entirely on the sequence-to-sequence model plus a bunch of other improvements over the last couple of years that addresses these problems at scale.

299
00:37:20,814 --> 00:37:23,780
So there's the model, you know, it's a very complicated model.

300
00:37:24,078 --> 00:37:28,484
It uses eight GPUs to train this very deep LSTM model.

301
00:37:28,960 --> 00:37:32,420
It has an attention model that's been an interesting area to add.

302
00:37:33,040 --> 00:37:34,596
and then it has a decoder.

303
00:37:35,502 --> 00:37:42,276
and then it uses data parallelism to essentially speed up training.

304
00:37:42,990 --> 00:37:51,364
and we're actually able to get to significantly close the gap between the old production system and human translations.

305
00:37:51,982 --> 00:37:59,364
So in some cases, we're able to close the gap by 87% between the old system and human quality translation systems.

306
00:37:59,854 --> 00:38:01,056
And this is really, really important.

307
00:38:01,216 --> 00:38:05,380
Those kinds of translation quality improvements don't come along basically ever.

308
00:38:05,696 --> 00:38:09,060
And here we're able to actually really significantly improve it.

309
00:38:09,536 --> 00:38:21,380
And we're seeing also another trend, I think, is more widespread use of transfer and multitask learning and zero shot learning, where you don't necessarily have a lot of data for some kinds of tasks you want to accomplish.

310
00:38:23,930 --> 00:38:29,636
And so currently most models are trained from scratch for a single task.

311
00:38:30,446 --> 00:38:31,684
And this is pretty inefficient.

312
00:38:31,950 --> 00:38:34,656
Uh data inefficient because you need a lot of data for each task.

313
00:38:34,736 --> 00:38:36,964
It's computation inefficient because you start from scratch.

314
00:38:37,454 --> 00:38:44,292
It's human sort of machine learning expert inefficient because each task that you try to solve takes a lot of effort.

315
00:38:45,806 --> 00:38:55,844
And so one of the things we were able to show as follow-on work to the initial translation release a couple weeks later was that we could actually do zero shot learning on how to translate languages.

316
00:38:56,142 --> 00:39:00,656
Language pairs we'd never observed before, we were actually able to translate.

317
00:39:00,816 --> 00:39:20,004
So we trained on language pairs of English to Korean and English to Japanese, shown in blue, and Korean to English and Japanese to English, but then this system was able to translate from language pairs it never observed, Japanese to Korean, Korean to Japanese, and produced pretty serviceable translations in that case.

318
00:39:20,750 --> 00:39:21,508
So that's pretty exciting.

319
00:39:22,958 --> 00:39:27,696
I think I'm going to skip some of this stuff and focus on automated machine learning.

320
00:39:27,856 --> 00:39:31,524
I think this is a really exciting area, and I'll close with this.

321
00:39:31,696 --> 00:39:37,924
So currently the way you solve problems is you have data and computation and you add machine learning expertise.

322
00:39:38,702 --> 00:39:46,948
And one of the things we'd like to be able to turn this into is can we take data and use a lot more computation, but eliminate a lot of the need for machine learning expertise?

323
00:39:47,726 --> 00:39:55,936
And I'll show you one example of this, which is a paper that's been submitted by our group to ICLR, called Neural Architecture Search with Reinforcement Learning.

324
00:39:56,016 --> 00:40:03,076
And the idea is we're going to generate a bunch of models, train them for a few hours, and then use the loss of the generated models as a reinforcement learning signal.

325
00:40:04,622 --> 00:40:10,690
And so these are the kinds of models it comes up with for some very small tasks, CFAR 10 and Penn Freebank.

326
00:40:11,456 --> 00:40:22,148
that's not a model that a human would probably come up with, but it actually comes very close to the state of the art result in CFAR 10 without any knowledge of what architectures to use.

327
00:40:22,606 --> 00:40:26,084
It just sort of explores architectures and figures out which ones work well and which ones don't.

328
00:40:26,670 --> 00:40:34,596
And similarly, for a language modeling task, that's a normal LSTM cell at the top, and this is the cell discovered by this architecture search.

329
00:40:35,022 --> 00:40:38,244
And this actually exceeds the state of the art on this particular modeling task.

330
00:40:40,302 --> 00:40:42,656
this is perplexity, so lower is better.

331
00:40:45,056 --> 00:40:48,996
finally, we need better computing facilities.

332
00:40:49,422 --> 00:40:50,736
neural nets have two properties.

333
00:40:50,816 --> 00:41:03,748
One is reduced precision is okay, the other is a handful of specific operations are make up the bulk of this computation, and that allows us to essentially specialize computations for doing machine learning and neural net computations.

334
00:41:04,206 --> 00:41:10,148
And so tensor processing units are a custom designed Google ASIC that accelerates neural net computations.

335
00:41:10,736 --> 00:41:12,676
they've been in production use for 20 months.

336
00:41:13,198 --> 00:41:18,116
They are used on every search query, they're used for all the translation work I talked about, they were used for the AlphaGo match.

337
00:41:23,374 --> 00:41:30,308
well, we still find uses for GPUs for training, for example, but this is a very good system for deploying large neural nets.

338
00:41:31,216 --> 00:41:41,604
Um so as we're able to accomplish more, the queries that we uh try to accomplish uh people expect computers to be able to do are getting more complicated.

339
00:41:42,062 --> 00:41:43,556
So here are some example queries.

340
00:41:44,462 --> 00:41:47,428
Which of these eye images shows symptoms of diabetic retinopathy?

341
00:41:48,206 --> 00:41:50,896
Actually, we can actually already do that one reasonably well.

