00:44:04,622 --> 00:44:05,956
Okay, great, thank you.

376
00:44:06,720 --> 00:44:15,940
And the third question is what do you do if you are not a large company like Google and don't have access to large data sets?

377
00:44:17,294 --> 00:44:25,284
so I think one of the things that's exciting is a lot of these data sets, a lot of these approaches can work well with transfer learning.

378
00:44:26,158 --> 00:44:33,348
there's cloud resources available in various cloud companies to run some of these computations if you don't have a large GPU cluster on your own.

379
00:44:33,838 --> 00:44:41,348
And so you can actually take, for example, in TensorFlow, we have a pre-trained image model that is trained on the ImageNet data set.

380
00:44:41,742 --> 00:44:56,708
Uh, you know, it used 100 GPUs for several weeks of training, but then you can take the result of that, a trained model, and there's a TensorFlow tutorial that shows you how to use the results of that model to classify images with different categories that you might care about.

381
00:44:56,880 --> 00:45:08,644
And you might have only a thousand images in that setup, and you can sort of pre-train with ImageNet, use our checkpoint, and then adopt sort of adapt the model for the thing you care about.

382
00:45:09,360 --> 00:45:09,680
Okay.

383
00:45:10,382 --> 00:45:18,052
Then the next question is what sort of AI products do you expect to be successful three to five years from now?

