00:47:11,440 --> 00:47:16,560
Recently, Google released this huge data set, 5 million YouTube data set.

404
00:47:16,880 --> 00:47:23,044
I just wanted to ask you if you can talk a little bit about that and how that has helped in your research with image recognition.

405
00:47:24,080 --> 00:47:24,400
Sure.

406
00:47:24,560 --> 00:47:30,080
So we Google released a large YouTube data set, and that's true.

407
00:47:30,240 --> 00:47:37,156
I think essentially the frontier of computer vision research is moving more into video rather than still images.

408
00:47:37,518 --> 00:47:51,460
And so having a large, you know, publicly available data set of that form will drive the computer perception research forward in the same way that the ImageNet data set drove it forward over the last sort of five or six years.

409
00:47:52,080 --> 00:47:58,680
I think we've now got a pretty good handle on still images, but we really don't quite know how to tackle video.

410
00:47:59,150 --> 00:48:06,000
You know, How should you integrate the temporal components into a model that really captures all the things you want?

411
00:48:06,254 --> 00:48:13,348
And so having a large data set for that is you know, an important precursor to the whole community doing research in that area.

412
00:48:16,238 --> 00:48:16,932
One last question.

413
00:48:24,000 --> 00:48:27,652
So you got a large work uh data already trained up.

414
00:48:27,982 --> 00:48:35,908
If I wanted to apply to a slightly different domain like financial services, so train it to get a lot more detail on companies and investment jargon.

415
00:48:36,334 --> 00:48:38,880
How do I deal with stability versus plasticity?

416
00:48:39,118 --> 00:48:44,100
I want to train it on the new concepts, but I don't necessarily want to break a lot of the previous learning.

417
00:48:44,654 --> 00:48:47,140
What would be an approach to try and balance that?

418
00:48:48,302 --> 00:48:48,612
Right.

419
00:48:48,960 --> 00:48:55,652
So you know, it's a fundamental question of how do you do transfer learning in any particular context.

420
00:48:55,840 --> 00:49:01,508
And typically, you want if you don't have enough data in your own domain, that's where transfer learning makes sense.

421
00:49:02,062 --> 00:49:05,444
If you have enough data on your own to solve the problem, then don't worry about it.

422
00:49:05,774 --> 00:49:25,280
But if you don't, then you want to initialize the state with training on a much larger data set that isn't exactly the one you care about, and then just start training on your new data set, on the data set you do care about where you don't have very much data, and essentially allow it as an initialization mechanism for that.

423
00:49:25,440 --> 00:49:26,596
That's typically the way you do it.

424
00:49:26,800 --> 00:49:29,316
There are other approaches, but that's the simplest, and I would try that first.

